-- Migration version 31 → 32: drop the mixed-case rows the Kilter own-climb
-- backfill left behind, so a device that already has them recovers.
--
-- 7.sqm settled that every persisted uuid is stored LOWER()'d and that BINARY
-- collation enforces identity; the catalogue importer lowercases at its
-- boundary for exactly that reason. The Kilter own-climb backfill, added
-- later, passed `climbUuid` through as the API spelled it. Its rows were
-- therefore the only mixed-case uuids in the board DB, and three things
-- followed from that:
--
--   * The importer's exists-gate compares the lowercased catalogue uuid
--     against them, misses, and inserts a SECOND row for the same climb.
--   * A logbook lookup tries the uuid as the API spelled it first and lands
--     on the backfill row rather than the catalogue one — which is why
--     downloading the catalogue repaired nothing.
--   * Those rows carry the two defects the backfill has since been fixed
--     for: a product SIZE stored in `layout_id`, and `climbConcat` HOLE ids
--     stored where placement ids belong. Both render the climb on the wrong
--     board with the wrong holds.
--
-- They are deleted rather than normalized in place. The importer skips a row
-- that already exists, so a lowercased-but-unchanged row would keep its wrong
-- layout and frames forever. Deleting is safe and self-repairing: the
-- catalogue re-inserts every climb it carries, and the next Kilter sync
-- re-creates the rest correctly — the backfill is idempotent and its
-- exists-gate is spelling-blind, so it will not duplicate what is already
-- there. Logbook entries keep their own rows throughout; they are in the
-- secure DB and only re-link.
--
-- No user-authored climb is touched: the editor (ClimbCreatorRepository) and
-- the community subscriber have always written lowercase.

DELETE FROM climb_stats WHERE climb_uuid IN (
    SELECT uuid FROM climbs WHERE uuid <> LOWER(uuid)
);

DELETE FROM climbs WHERE uuid <> LOWER(uuid);
